const body = `<h1>8.5c — Fog of War: Visibility Maps and Flood Fill</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: Line of Sight Map</h3>

    <p>Your dungeon map is a 2D array where 0=floor and 1=wall. The player stands
    at (playerRow, playerCol). Build a visibility map: a boolean 2D array where
    true means "the player can see this tile."</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">Map:
. . . # .
. . # . .
. P . . .    ← P at [2][1]
. . . . #
. # . . .

Visibility (radius 2, walls block):
. ✓ ✓ # .
✓ ✓ # . .
✓ P ✓ . .
✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ #
. # . . .</span></code></pre>

    <p>A tile is visible if:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>It's within the player's sight radius (Manhattan distance ≤ radius)</li>
        <li>There's an unblocked straight line between the player and the tile
            (Bresenham's line algorithm — check each cell on the line, stop at first wall)</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>Manhattan distance = |r1-r2| + |c1-c2|. For radius 2, how many tiles
        are within range (not counting walls)? Draw a diamond shape around the
        player. Now: why is Manhattan distance alone not enough? Consider a tile
        at [0][3] with a wall at [0][2]. It's within range 2, but can the player
        see it?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: Flood Fill Reveal</h3>

    <p>Instead of fixed-radius vision, use flood fill: the player can see every
    tile that's connected by a clear path (no walls) AND within the sight radius.
    This handles winding corridors correctly — you can't see through walls, but
    you CAN see around corners.</p>

    <p>Implement a recursive flood fill starting from the player's position:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>If tile is out of bounds → stop</li>
        <li>If tile is a wall → mark visible but stop flooding past it</li>
        <li>If tile is already visited → stop</li>
        <li>If tile exceeds sight radius → stop</li>
        <li>Mark visible, recurse to 4 neighbors with radius-1</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Infinite Recursion</h3>
        <p>Without a visited check, flood fill will visit [r][c], then [r+1][c],
        then [r][c] again, forever. Where do you put the visited check — before
        or after the recursive calls? Test on a 2×2 empty room. Count how many
        times your recursive method is called. Can you make it fewer?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: RPG Integration — Fog of War Overlay</h3>

    <p>Modify your dungeon renderer to print hidden tiles as "." (unexplored) and
    visible tiles as their actual content ("#" for walls, " " for floor, "P" for
    player). Every time the player moves, recompute visibility and redraw.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">Before move:         After move right:
. . . . .           . . . . .
. . P . .           . . P . .
. . # . .           # # # . .
. . . . .           . . . . .

(radius=1, wall blocks)  (moved one right, wall now visible)</span></code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Memory Map</h3>
        <p>Split the map into two layers: <em>explored</em> (tiles the player has
        ever seen) and <em>visible</em> (tiles the player currently sees).
        Explored tiles stay revealed permanently but dimmed. Only currently-visible
        tiles show live updates (monsters moving, items appearing). This is the
        difference between "fog of war" (you forget what was there) and "memory"
        (you remember the layout but not the current contents).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on to Unit 9, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Compute a visibility map using flood fill with a sight radius</li>
            <li>Handle walls correctly — visible but impassable</li>
            <li>Avoid infinite recursion with visited tracking</li>
            <li>Integrate fog of war into the dungeon renderer</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/dungeon-map.html">Dungeon Map Builder</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-traversal.html">2D Array Traversal</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/recursion-basics.html">Recursion Basics</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
